export abstract anchor FactorySecurity:
    authorizationMatrix:
        Authorization is deny-by-default on operation, actor, target, and
        precondition. Reads are authorized reads under the read filters, not
        operations.
        Order and plan changes are allowed to the engineer or the plan owner
        within revision authority. Execution reports, help requests, evidence,
        and progress are allowed to the assigned agent for its step. Engineer
        questions are answered by the engineer or an explicitly delegated agent
        within its grant. Configuration and evolution changes require the
        engineer or a recorded pre-authorization. Delivery is performed by the
        delivery owner, merger, or integrator for the target; acceptance by an
        independent assessor. Auditors perform no operation.
        Cancellation uses cause authority: supersession by code with an
        authorized revision, budget and policy by code, engineer replacement by
        the engineer, higher-tier cancellation by a higher tier within its own
        order or subtree. The cause authority also sets grace. A request to
        cancel and the actual stop are separate permissions. A harness adapter
        may confirm a stop but may not decide cancellation.
    actorIdentity:
        An actor context is signed by the project authentication service with
        audience, nonce, and freshness, and verified at the transport before
        any operation. Session and attempt records store role and tier.
        A free-text actor field is never sufficient. A verification failure is
        denied and audited, distinct from an absent context.
    capabilityRecord:
        A capability records issuer, operation, target, scope, expiry, and
        revocation. The issuer must itself hold the scope; delegation cannot
        widen it. Capabilities are signed and written only by factory code.
        They are checked at intent commit and before every external effect. If a
        capability is revoked or expires before the effect, the intent is
        tombstoned, no effect occurs, and the incident escalates.
    auditIntegrity:
        A change audit entry commits in the change transaction. Denial and
        replay entries are separate append-only audit entries with their own
        sequence and no state change other than the audit append.
        Entries are hash-chained per target and globally. Checkpoints are
        append-only, monotonic, independently readable, and anchored outside
        the writable store. A maximum checkpoint interval bounds undetected
        tampering. An unreachable anchor marks audit degraded and blocks new
        external effects until it recovers; existing work continues.
        A broken chain or missing sequence blocks the affected target or the
        whole factory when the global chain is affected. The incident is
        recorded outside the broken chain where possible. Recovery quarantines
        the affected scope; it never rewrites history.
    authorizationRows:
        Each operation row names its actors, target, and preconditions. Submit
        order: engineer or an agent with a recorded engineer-delegation
        capability; precondition a valid submission. Revise order: engineer or
        the recorded plan owner or a higher tier; precondition an active order
        and the current aggregate version. Add or revise step or dependency:
        plan owner or higher tier, or the assigned agent for its own work within
        revision authority; precondition an active plan and current plan
        version. Supersede: plan owner or higher tier; precondition a committed
        authorized revision and a nonterminal step. Propose or merge a plan
        revision: plan owner or higher tier and the merge owner; precondition
        the current plan version.
        Report outcome, request help, escalate, attach evidence, report
        progress, heartbeat, and record build or check: assigned agent;
        precondition an active attempt and the session lease. Answer or
        withdraw a question: engineer or delegated agent; precondition the
        current question generation. Assess a step: independent assessor;
        precondition the result revision and frozen criteria. Accept an order:
        independent assessor; precondition all required receipts, combined
        checks, and the completion assessment, and no self-implementation.
        Request cancellation: cause authority; precondition the target attempt.
        Stop: cause authority and the adapter; precondition an existing request
        and grace. Configuration and evolution: engineer or recorded
        pre-authorization with separate grants. Delivery: delivery owner,
        merger, or integrator for the target; precondition the target lease,
        epoch, and candidate revision. Dispatch and resource allocation: factory
        code. Start, shutdown, and import: engineer or factory code. Run
        conformance and append a report: an independent verifier principal;
        precondition an admitted independence record. Alter an existing report
        or write implementation code: denied. Reads use read filters.
        The plan owner is a durable role on the plan record; after a merge it is
        the merge owner, and after recovery it is recorded with the plan. Higher
        tier is relative to that role.
    auditSequence:
        Every audit entry has one global sequence and a sequence for its primary
        chain. The primary chain is the canonical target, otherwise the order,
        account, or factory scope. Every entry links the global hash and its
        chain hash. Denial and replay entries allocate both sequences and follow
        the same links. A multi-key change records one entry with a primary
        chain and references the other keys.
        Checkpoints are post-commit and bind a global position to every chain
        head at that position. A chain head ahead of the checkpoint position is
        unresolved, not tampering. The anchor is append-only and independently
        readable; last-seen checkpoint state lives in the anchor, not the
        writable store. An interval breach blocks new external effects by effect
        class; an effect already in flight becomes in-doubt.
    audit:
        Change entries commit in the change transaction. They are gap-free and
        ordered by one global sequence plus a chain sequence. Denial and replay
        entries follow auditIntegrity. Recovery reads committed records and
        never replays the audit to rebuild state.
    authorizationConformance:
        Every authorization row maps to at least one positive cell and one
        denial cell, and a missing row is a failure. Cells keep the operation
        and precondition constant and vary actor or target; a cross-order cell
        records the target change as the tested dimension. Each cell asserts the
        denial reason, the gate exercised, the order aggregate version
        unchanged, and a canonical SHA-256 state digest over the order, attempt,
        result, lease, and claim records unchanged. Audit cells require a
        populated log, the agent's own entries present, a clean global and
        per-chain validation, per-chain tampering with the affected scope
        blocked while a contemporaneous clean control validates, one effect for
        a replay, and an unreachable anchor blocking new effects. Not-applicable
        is not a pass.
    securityConformance:
        Separately verdicted cells pair a positive control and a denial:
        agent cross-order action denied while the same agent acts on its own
        step; agent answer denied while the engineer or a delegated agent
        answers; agent cancel above tier denied while a higher tier within its
        subtree cancels; capability expired or revoked at the effect gate denied
        while a fresh capability succeeds; wrong-account or wrong-route
        credential denied while the correct binding succeeds; unauthorized
        configuration change denied while an authorized change succeeds;
        implementer self-acceptance denied while an independent assessor
        accepts; and dashboard action without identity denied while the same
        action with identity succeeds.
        Audit cells assert an agent can read its own entries and cannot read
        another order's, a clean chain and checkpoint validate, a denial entry
        exists with no state change beyond the audit append, a replay appends
        one entry, and a tampered chain or checkpoint mismatch is detected and
        blocks the affected scope. A cell that never reaches its condition is
        not passed; unsupported and not-applicable are not passing verdicts for
        a claimed factory.
